      "The Middle colonies had productive farms and busy ports, including Philadelphia and New York. Dutch, English, German, and other communities brought different religions and languages. Pennsylvania’s Quaker leaders supported greater religious tolerance. Southern colonies had longer growing seasons and large cash-crop plantations alongside smaller farms. Virginia’s House of Burgesses was an elected assembly, a group making laws. Carolina’s proprietors also held power over its assembly. Slavery existed in all three regions, but plantation agriculture made enslaved labor especially central in the South.",
